{"subjectId":"deep-learning","id":"backpropagation","title":"Backpropagation","group":"Training Neural Networks","order":7,"html":"<p class=\"lead\">Backpropagation computes the gradient of the loss with respect to every weight in one backward sweep, reusing intermediate results via the chain rule. It's what makes training deep networks affordable.</p>\n<div class=\"callout callout--def\"><p><strong>Backpropagation</strong> — reverse-mode automatic differentiation: run the forward pass and remember the intermediate values, then walk the computational graph backwards, multiplying each node's <strong>local gradient</strong> by the gradient flowing in from above.</p></div>\n<div class=\"formula\">chain rule: ∂L/∂w = ∂L/∂a · ∂a/∂z · ∂z/∂w</div>\n<section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-sitemap\"></i>A computational graph by hand</h3>\n<p>One neuron with a squared-error loss: <code>z = w·x + b</code>, <code>a = σ(z)</code>, <code>L = (a − y)²</code>.</p>\n<div class=\"flow\"><div class=\"flow-node\">x, w, b</div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\">z = w·x + b</div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\">a = σ(z)</div><div class=\"flow-arrow\">→</div><div class=\"flow-node\">L = (a − y)²</div></div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import math\n\nx, y, w, b = 2.0, 1.0, 0.5, -0.5\n# forward\nz = w * x + b                           # 0.5\na = 1 / (1 + math.exp(-z))              # σ(0.5)\nL = (a - y) ** 2\n# backward — one local derivative per edge, multiplied right to left\ndL_da = 2 * (a - y)\nda_dz = a * (1 - a)\ndL_dz = dL_da * da_dz\ndL_dw = dL_dz * x                       # ∂z/∂w = x\ndL_db = dL_dz * 1                       # ∂z/∂b = 1\nprint(round(L, 4), round(dL_dw, 4), round(dL_db, 4))   # 0.1425 -0.3549 -0.1774</code></pre></figure>\n<p>PyTorch's autograd does exactly this bookkeeping for you:</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import torch\n\nx, y = torch.tensor(2.0), torch.tensor(1.0)\nw = torch.tensor(0.5, requires_grad=True)\nb = torch.tensor(-0.5, requires_grad=True)\n\nL = (torch.sigmoid(w * x + b) - y) ** 2\nL.backward()                            # fills w.grad and b.grad\nprint(round(w.grad.item(), 4), round(b.grad.item(), 4))   # -0.3549 -0.1774</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-code\"></i>A 2-layer network from scratch</h3>\n<p>Forward: <code>H = relu(X W₁ + b₁)</code>, <code>P = σ(H W₂ + b₂)</code>, BCE loss. Backward, the key results are: for sigmoid + BCE, <code>∂L/∂logits = (P − y) / n</code>; for a linear layer <code>Y = XW</code>, <code>∂L/∂W = Xᵀ · ∂L/∂Y</code> and <code>∂L/∂X = ∂L/∂Y · Wᵀ</code>; for ReLU, pass the gradient where the input was positive.</p>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import numpy as np\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nX = np.array([[0, 0], [0, 1], [1, 0], [1, 1]], dtype=float)\ny = np.array([[0], [1], [1], [0]], dtype=float)              # XOR\n\nparams = {\"W1\": rng.normal(0, 1, (2, 8)), \"b1\": np.full(8, 0.1),\n          \"W2\": rng.normal(0, 1, (8, 1)), \"b2\": np.zeros(1)}\n\ndef forward_backward(p):\n    Z1 = X @ p[\"W1\"] + p[\"b1\"]; H = np.maximum(0, Z1)       # forward\n    Z2 = H @ p[\"W2\"] + p[\"b2\"]; P = 1 / (1 + np.exp(-Z2))\n    loss = -np.mean(y * np.log(P) + (1 - y) * np.log(1 - P))\n    dZ2 = (P - y) / len(X)                                   # backward\n    dW2, db2 = H.T @ dZ2, dZ2.sum(0)\n    dH = dZ2 @ p[\"W2\"].T\n    dZ1 = dH * (Z1 &gt; 0)\n    dW1, db1 = X.T @ dZ1, dZ1.sum(0)\n    return loss, {\"W1\": dW1, \"b1\": db1, \"W2\": dW2, \"b2\": db2}\n\nloss, grads = forward_backward(params)\nprint(round(loss, 4))                     # 0.7371 ← before training</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-checks\"></i>Gradient checking</h3>\n<p>Always verify a hand-written backward pass numerically. Nudge each weight by <code>±ε</code> and compare the slope of the loss with the analytic gradient:</p>\n<div class=\"formula\">∂L/∂θ ≈ [ L(θ + ε) − L(θ − ε) ] / 2ε relative error = |g_analytic − g_numeric| / (|g_analytic| + |g_numeric|)</div>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import numpy as np\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nX = np.array([[0, 0], [0, 1], [1, 0], [1, 1]], dtype=float)\ny = np.array([[0], [1], [1], [0]], dtype=float)\nparams = {\"W1\": rng.normal(0, 1, (2, 8)), \"b1\": np.full(8, 0.1),\n          \"W2\": rng.normal(0, 1, (8, 1)), \"b2\": np.zeros(1)}\n\ndef forward_backward(p):\n    Z1 = X @ p[\"W1\"] + p[\"b1\"]; H = np.maximum(0, Z1)\n    Z2 = H @ p[\"W2\"] + p[\"b2\"]; P = 1 / (1 + np.exp(-Z2))\n    loss = -np.mean(y * np.log(P) + (1 - y) * np.log(1 - P))\n    dZ2 = (P - y) / len(X)\n    dH = dZ2 @ p[\"W2\"].T\n    dZ1 = dH * (Z1 &gt; 0)\n    return loss, {\"W1\": X.T @ dZ1, \"b1\": dZ1.sum(0), \"W2\": H.T @ dZ2, \"b2\": dZ2.sum(0)}\n\n_, grads = forward_backward(params)\neps, worst = 1e-6, 0.0\nfor name, value in params.items():\n    for i in np.ndindex(value.shape):\n        old = value[i]\n        value[i] = old + eps; up, _ = forward_backward(params)\n        value[i] = old - eps; down, _ = forward_backward(params)\n        value[i] = old\n        numeric, analytic = (up - down) / (2 * eps), grads[name][i]\n        if abs(numeric) + abs(analytic) &gt; 0:\n            worst = max(worst, abs(numeric - analytic) / (abs(numeric) + abs(analytic)))\nprint(worst &lt; 1e-6)            # True ← analytic and numeric gradients agree</code></pre></figure>\n</section><section class=\"block\"><h3><i class=\"ti ti-player-play\"></i>Training it</h3>\n<figure data-lang=\"py\" class=\"code\"><figcaption>Python</figcaption><pre><code>import numpy as np\n\nrng = np.random.default_rng(0)\nX = np.array([[0, 0], [0, 1], [1, 0], [1, 1]], dtype=float)\ny = np.array([[0], [1], [1], [0]], dtype=float)\np = {\"W1\": rng.normal(0, 1, (2, 8)), \"b1\": np.full(8, 0.1),\n     \"W2\": rng.normal(0, 1, (8, 1)), \"b2\": np.zeros(1)}\n\ndef forward_backward(p):\n    Z1 = X @ p[\"W1\"] + p[\"b1\"]; H = np.maximum(0, Z1)\n    Z2 = H @ p[\"W2\"] + p[\"b2\"]; P = 1 / (1 + np.exp(-Z2))\n    loss = -np.mean(y * np.log(P) + (1 - y) * np.log(1 - P))\n    dZ2 = (P - y) / len(X)\n    dH = dZ2 @ p[\"W2\"].T\n    dZ1 = dH * (Z1 &gt; 0)\n    return loss, P, {\"W1\": X.T @ dZ1, \"b1\": dZ1.sum(0), \"W2\": H.T @ dZ2, \"b2\": dZ2.sum(0)}\n\nhistory = []\nfor step in range(2000):\n    loss, P, grads = forward_backward(p)\n    for k in p:\n        p[k] -= 0.5 * grads[k]                  # plain gradient descent\n    if step % 500 == 0:\n        history.append(round(float(loss), 3))\nprint(history)                                  # [0.737, 0.003, 0.001, 0.001] ← loss every 500 steps\nprint((P &gt; 0.5).astype(int).ravel().tolist())   # [0, 1, 1, 0] ← XOR learned</code></pre></figure>\n<div class=\"callout callout--note\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Key point</div><p>Cost: the backward pass costs roughly <strong>2×</strong> the forward pass, and it needs the stored activations — which is why training uses much more memory than inference.</p></div>\n<div class=\"callout callout--warn\"><div class=\"callout-label\">Watch out</div><p>Gradient checking is for debugging only — it costs two forward passes per parameter. Use <code>float64</code>, a small <code>ε</code> (~1e-6) and avoid points where ReLU is exactly at its kink.</p></div>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Explain backpropagation in two sentences.</summary><div><p>After a forward pass that stores intermediate values, we apply the chain rule backwards from the loss: each node multiplies the incoming gradient by its local derivative and passes it to its inputs. This gives the gradient for every parameter in about the cost of one extra forward pass.</p></div></details>\n<details class=\"qa\"><summary>Why reverse mode and not forward mode differentiation?</summary><div><p>A network has millions of inputs (parameters) and one scalar output (the loss). Reverse mode gets all parameter gradients in one backward pass; forward mode would need one pass per parameter.</p></div></details></section>"}